[brief] This is a rough estimate. The tools did not return a count of 2025 galaxy cluster papers, so the figure combines an approximate count of all astro-ph papers with a topic share that I assumed. Step 1, 
**Survey** — 9 tools on 1 servers, 3 papers found.

This is a rough estimate. The tools did not return a count of 2025 galaxy cluster papers, so the figure combines an approximate count of all astro-ph papers with a topic share that I assumed. Step 1, the baseline: an unverified web snippet says the Open Journal of Astrophysics published 213 papers in 2025, 'just over 1%' of astro-ph submissions. That puts astro-ph at roughly 18,000-21,000 new papers in 2025 (excluding replacements, possibly including cross-lists), which matches the growth described in 2602.12303 ('Astrophysics Wrapped 2025') and the size of the 408,590-paper corpus covering 1992 to July 2025 (2511.12353). Step 2, the cluster share, which is my own assumption and not from any source: papers mainly about galaxy cluster physics (intracluster medium in X-ray and SZ, cluster cosmology and mass calibration, cluster lensing, radio halos and relics, cluster mergers, brightest cluster galaxies and intracluster light, cluster galaxy evolution, protoclusters) are probably about 5-8% of astro-ph, mostly in astro-ph.CO and astro-ph.GA. That gives about 1,000-1,600 papers. Counting any paper where clusters are an important secondary topic (group-scale work, cluster-calibrated cosmology, simulations with cluster analyses, high-energy work using clusters as targets) gives about 2,000-3,000. Central estimate: about 1,500 arXiv papers in 2025 on galaxy cluster physics under a strict definition (plausible range 1,000-2,500), and up to about 3,000 under a broad definition. Large 2025 data releases and follow-up work (eROSITA, Euclid, ACT/SPT, LOFAR, XRISM, JWST protoclusters) probably make 2025 a high-output year, which argues for the upper half of the strict range.

Quantities worth checking: Total new astro-ph submissions in 2025, primary category versus including cross-lists (arXiv annual report, or the arXiv listing API by date); Number of 2025 astro-ph papers whose title or abstract matches 'galaxy cluster', 'cluster of galaxies', 'intracluster', 'ICM', 'protocluster', 'radio halo/relic', 'BCG' (excluding star clusters and globular clusters); The same query run for 2020-2024, to check the growth trend and that the 2025 number is reasonable; Share of cluster-tagged papers in the AstroMLab concept dataset for January-July 2025, scaled to the full year; Strict versus broad definition: how many papers have clusters as the main topic versus a secondary mention.
