[directions] d-01 [open] Produce a sourced, rough estimate of how many 2025 arXiv pap; d-02 [open] Independently derive the share of galaxy-cluster papers and 
**Plan** — 2 lines of investigation:
1. Produce a sourced, rough estimate of how many 2025 arXiv papers concern galaxy cluster physics, under a strict and a broad definition. Replace the survey's assumed 5-8% topic share and its unverified total astro-ph count with numbers quoted from papers wherever the literature allows. — The survey estimate (about 1,500 strict, range 1,000-2,500; up to about 3,000 broad) rests on two weak inputs: an unverified web snippet for total astro-ph volume, and an assumed cluster share. 'Astrophysics Wrapped 2025' (2602.12303) and AstroMLab 5 (2511.12353) are full-text sources that may state both quantities directly, as a 2025 paper count and as subfield or concept statistics. With no ADS or arXiv-API counting tool available, this paper-based route is the most direct one. One direction is enough because both inputs come from the same few papers.
2. Independently derive the share of galaxy-cluster papers and the 2020-2025 trend from AstroMLab 5 (2511.12353) and other citable bibliometric sources, as a cross-check on d-01. — d-01 depends heavily on 2602.12303. A second, separately budgeted check on the cluster share (concept-level tagging over 408k papers) and on the per-year growth is the only way to test the assumed 5-8% share and the high-output-year claim. It also frees d-01's budget. (added by the critic)
Critic's view: revise — The plan does not fit the 23-call budget. Two full-text downloads, several read_arxiv_chunk passes per paper, possible figure extraction, a conditional external search, six sanity-check queries and multiple cite() calls add up to well over 23. Split the work: d-01 covers 2602.12303 and the final synthesis; the added direction covers 2511.12353 and the independent anchors. Cap the sanity-check queries at about 3.; There is no fallback for the likely case that 2602.12303 gives subfield or category shares but no galaxy-cluster share. Define the proxy now: (astro-ph.CO + astro-ph.GA + astro-ph.HE share) x (cluster fraction within those categories). Also state how the within-category fraction will be justified, for example from a quoted concept or topic table, or failing that as a labelled assumption with a range. Otherwise the final number simply repeats the survey's 5-8% assumption.; The brief asks for a 2020-2024 trend check, and the plan has no step for it. Add one: extract per-year astro-ph totals, and per-year cluster or concept counts if available, from either paper. Use them to test the survey's claim that 2025 was a high-output year, rather than asserting it from the data-release list (eROSITA, Euclid, XRISM)..
