**Completed — Produce a sourced, rough estimate of how many 2025 arXiv papers concern galaxy cluster physics, under a strict and a broad definition. Replace the survey's assumed 5-8% topic share and its unverified total astro-ph count with numbers quoted from papers wherever the literature allows..** Sourced base (f-001, high confidence): arXiv:2602.12303 (Astrophysics Wrapped 2025, Sec. 2.1) gives 18,660 astro-ph papers for 2025. Quote: 'This does not include replacement submissions ... The dataset also does not include cross-submissions'. The base is therefore primary-category papers only, which answers q-01. This is the low end of the survey's unverified 18,000-21,000. Sourced split (f-002, Table 1 of 2602.12303): GA 4,761; HE 3,869; SR 2,958; CO 2,789; EP 2,293; IM 1,722; general 268. GA plus CO is 40.5% of the total; GA plus CO plus HE is 61.2%. Neither 2602.12303 nor AstroMLab 5 (arXiv:2511.12353) gives a numerical galaxy-cluster share (dead end x-01). 2602.12303 gives keyword and subfield statistics only as figures. Its Figs. 12 and 13 were extracted to the run directory (/Users/nramachandra/hep-genesis-testing/projects/ramp-features/runs/261015_224454/figures_2602.12303/) but not read numerically. 2511.12353 gives only example concepts and the aggregate for 1992 to July 2025, with no per-year counts. Its ADS keyword table lists 'galaxies: evolution' at 5.5% and 'dark matter' at 3.0%, with no cluster row. The cluster share is therefore still an assumption, applied per category on top of the sourced split (f-003, low confidence): CO 15-25%, GA 8-12%, HE 3-5%, other categories about 30-80 papers. That gives a strict estimate of about 950-1,550 papers, central about 1,200 (about 6.5% of 18,660). The broad estimate, which adds papers where clusters are a secondary topic plus gr-qc/hep-ph papers cross-listed into astro-ph, is about 2,000-3,000. The survey's central figure of about 1,500 survives, at the upper edge of the strict range; a central value near 1,200 is better supported by the confirmed primary-only base of 18,660. The abstract searches mostly returned galaxy-cluster papers but also star clusters, so keyword counts must exclude star clusters. Limitations: the full-text files are 2602.12303.txt and 2511.12353.txt in the run directory. No cite() calls were made because the budget ran out, so the verbatim quotes are recorded in the finding notes but not yet validated in references.bib. Open: q-05 asks for a measured share, either by reading Figs. 12-13 numerically or by running the ADS query.
